@path mentions and /add now respect --deny-dir/--allow-dir, judged at the file's resolved target. Committed as 9348ada5 ("Part of #1002"). Build, all tests, clippy with -D warnings and the fmt check pass.

Reproduced first. I set up a temp fixture with proj/notes.md -> ../secret/notes.md. Before the fix, both @<link> and /add <link> put the secret token into the content sent to the model. Neither function took the fence as an input, so --deny-dir could never apply there.

The fix. The check is in the two functions that actually read the file, not in each command. read_file_for_add and read_image_for_add now take the session's DirectoryRestrictions and call admit_for_add before reading anything. That uses the same check_path resolver as #996 and #1002, so symlinks are judged where they point. Because the parameter is required, the compiler made every caller pass the fence. That turned up two callers the task didn't list but which send the same bytes:
• /explain in dispatch.rs
• --image in main.rs (both of its code paths)

What the user sees. A refused file prints a yellow warning naming the path and the fence entry, and the file is not sent. Its @mention stays as typed, and the rest of the prompt and any other mentions still go through. A failed read (missing file, permissions) keeps the old red ✗. The fence code lives in a new file, src/commands_file_fence.rs.

Tests (src/commands_file_fence_tests.rs, 12 tests, all in temp dirs):
• **Refused:** a symlink into a denied dir, a direct path into a denied dir, and an image. Each case checks every command that can reach that file. Warning lines are checked whole with assert_eq!, and the token never appears.
• **Allow-list:** a link inside the allowed dir that points outside it is refused.
• Unchanged: an allowed file, an allowed symlink, and a session with no fence give exactly the same output as before.
• Positive control: I disabled admit_for_add so it always allowed, in one break → test → restore command. Exactly the 8 refusal tests failed and the other 4 stayed green. After restoring, 12/12 pass, and no sabotage marker is left in the code.

Other changes
• I added one sentence to docs/src/configuration/permissions.md, which already lists what the fence covers, and a paragraph to the commands_file.rs entry in ARCHITECTURE.md.
• I raised the `commands_file.rs size limit in tests/module_size.rs` from 2804 to 2854 lines, to record the growth. Moving the fence code into its own file kept it to +50 lines instead of about +200.

Not done: there's still no size cap on inlined files, which was out of scope as planned. I didn't comment on #1002 because the bug reproduced; the plan only called for a comment if it didn't.
